<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jin Shuren</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1879">1879</a>–<a href="/years/1941">1941</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jin Shuren was a Chinese official who governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a> from 1928, after the assassination of Yang Zengxin, until 1933. His abolition of the Kumul Khanate in 1930, heavy taxes and settlement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-chinese">Han Chinese</a> provoked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumul-rebellion">Kumul Rebellion</a>, which spread across the province and drew in the Hui warlord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ma-zhongying">Ma Zhongying</a>. He was overthrown in a coup in April 1933 and fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> proper.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jin-shuren.jpg" alt="Jin Shuren" width="320" height="419" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jin_Shuren_(cropped).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Jin Shuren was born in Yongjing, in Hezhou in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu">Gansu</a>. After graduating from the Gansu provincial academy he served as principal of the provincial normal school and then entered the imperial civil service, where he came to the notice of Yang Zengxin, then district magistrate of Hezhou. When Yang became Governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a> in 1908, Jin followed him as a county magistrate, and after the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a> in 1911 he rose under Yang's absolute rule. In 1927 he was made Provincial Commissioner for Civil Affairs in Ürümqi.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>When Yang was assassinated in July 1928, Jin took his place and asked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a> government in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanking</a> to recognise him. It did so with new titles, Provincial Chairman and Commander-in-Chief. Jin enlarged the secret police, doubled the pay of the army and police and bought new weapons. He kept the old administration but, like Yang, appointed relatives and men from his own province, replacing officials from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-chinese">Han Chinese</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu">Gansu</a> and above all from Hezhou; one brother, Jin Shuhsin, became commissioner for military affairs. He also introduced internal passports, which tightened control of travel and raised revenue.</p><p>His rule was marked by corruption and repression. He confiscated local lands for redistribution but gave them to his own associates, and in 1932 his officials obstructed the French Yellow Expedition. By signing an arms treaty with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> without approval he angered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a>, which ordered the Hui general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ma-zhongying">Ma Zhongying</a>, whose troops became the 36th Division of the National Revolutionary Army, to overthrow him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Revolts spread against his government, and in April 1933, after the First Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urumqi">Urumqi</a>, his White Russian troops under Colonel Pavel Pappengut turned against him and ended his rule. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sheng-shicai">Sheng Shicai</a> succeeded him. In October 1933 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a> arrested Jin; he was tried in March 1935 and sentenced to three and a half years in prison, but was pardoned on 10 October 1935 and released the next day. He died in 1941.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jin Shuren” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The abolition of the Kumul Khanate in 1930 provoked revolts across Xinjiang against Governor <b>Jin Shuren</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/first-east-turkestan-republic">First East Turkestan Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>After the death of the last khan, Maqsud Shah, in 1930, the Xinjiang governor <b>Jin Shuren</b> abolished the khanate and imposed direct rule and new taxes.</blockquote><a href="/state/kumul-khanate">Kumul Khanate</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Yongjing County, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lanzhou">Lanzhou</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.94,103.32&amp;year=1919" aria-label="Yongjing County on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7155 866 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Jin Shuren"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7605 1136h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7605 1136h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=35.94,103.32&amp;year=1919">What was here in 1919? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
